WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.440
The clock chip's whine has learned to hop,
between nineteen hundred,

2
00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.340
two thousand and twenty-one hundred hertz.

3
00:00:05.660 --> 00:00:07.540
So the team builds three narrow notches,

4
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.240
one per whine,
and multiplies them into one rule.

5
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.600
On the laptop it's perfect.

6
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:13.900
On a cheap eight-bit chip,

7
00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.100
one click starts a hum
that grows every sample,

8
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.680
slams into the chip's limits,
and swings between them forever.

9
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.800
Same rule, same numbers. Or are they?

10
00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:23.830
Every number in that rule
is a coefficient,

11
00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.290
a fixed number a sample
gets multiplied by.

12
00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:28.130
The chip stores each one in eight bits:

13
00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.350
a whole number of steps,

14
00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.090
two hundred fifty-six choices.

15
00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.530
Rounding to the nearest step
is called quantization.

16
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:36.040
Try lesson six's notch first.

17
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.620
Its coefficients are under one,

18
00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.520
so the chip can use a fine step,
one over a hundred twenty-eight.

19
00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:42.400
Point eight one becomes
a hundred and four steps,

20
00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:43.560
point eight one two five.

21
00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.160
The poles move out
by point zero zero one four. That's all.

22
00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:49.350
Now the three-notch rule.

23
00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.590
Its biggest coefficient
is two point eight six.

24
00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:54.450
A step of one over sixty-four
only reaches just under two,

25
00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.350
so the chip is forced
to one over thirty-two,

26
00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:57.730
for every coefficient.

27
00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.310
Each lands about a hundredth off.

28
00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:00.310
Small, you'd think.

29
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.660
Find the rounded rule's poles.

30
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:03.840
The middle pair moves in.

31
00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.860
The outer pairs jump
outside the unit circle,

32
00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.280
to one point oh five seven,

33
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.900
so an echo grows
almost six percent per sample.

34
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.360
The chip's rule isn't your rule.

35
00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.400
It's a neighbor,
and that neighbor isn't stable.

36
00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:14.960
But why?

37
00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:16.320
Next to lesson six,

38
00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.220
these errors
are five and a half times bigger,

39
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.640
yet the poles moved
seventy to a hundred times as far.

40
00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:21.640
The book's answer:

41
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.080
a pole moves by the error over the product
of its distances to the other poles.

42
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.100
One over that product is its multiplier.

43
00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.740
Here, three poles are crammed
together near the top.

44
00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:34.000
The middle one's neighbors are just
point zero seven seven away.

45
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.700
Oh, its multiplier is twenty-two.

46
00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.080
Lesson six's lone pair
sits one point eight apart,

47
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.840
a multiplier of point five six.

48
00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.120
Its error shrinks.

49
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:45.020
Multiplying out crowds
all six poles into one rule,

50
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.840
each coefficient a blend of all of them.

51
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:47.820
So don't multiply.

52
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.200
Build one notch at a time:
a second-order section,

53
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.500
one zero pair and one pole pair,

54
00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.460
where each number
is something you can see.

55
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.480
Run three in a row.

56
00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:55.420
That's a cascade.

57
00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:57.820
Each section is rounded alone,

58
00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.600
at a step of one over sixty-four.

59
00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.890
Every pole lands at point nine seven six,
safely inside.

60
00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.320
The whine
keeps one point three percent at most,

61
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:06.680
the voice ninety-nine and a half.

62
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.700
Tiny errors aren't always tiny trouble.

63
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.640
Stability is a line,
and crowded poles get pushed across it.

64
00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:15.970
The chips go out,
and the whine is gone for good.

65
00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:19.030
Then the wideband line starts leaking
a faint ripple of high tones,

66
00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.210
through a filter
that's already ideal on paper.
